### 4\. Permission manager me permissive logic

File:

`app/src/main/java/com/example/domain/security/TaskzPermissionManager.kt`

Isme ek important function hai:

`matchesScope()`

Kuch conditions is tarah hain:

```kotlin
scope.centreId == null || centreId == null || scope.centreId == centreId
```

Aur:

```kotlin
scope.cityId == null || cityId == null || scope.cityId == cityId
```

Iska matlab agar user ka scope ya task ka scope ID null hai, to match `true` ho sakta hai.

Example:

* IT Manager Centre A ka hai.

* Kisi task ka centre ID null hai.

* Code task ko scope match de sakta hai.

Ek aur problem:

```kotlin
if (targetScope == ScopeLevel.GLOBAL) return true
```

Isse client permission manager global scope ko broadly accessible maan sakta hai.

### Aur kya problematic hai?

* `canCreateTask()` sab active users ke liye true return karta hai.

* `canCreateIncident()` bhi sab active users ke liye true return karta hai.

* `canCompleteTask()` assigned-user check ke alawa broad scope checks karta hai.

* `canEscalateIncident()` resolved incident ka status check karta hai, lekin actual backend RPC bhi ye enforce kare, ye zaroori hai.

Android checks security boundary nahi hain, lekin UI ko bhi accurate permission model follow karna chahiye.

Required fix: missing scope IDs ko wildcard mat maano. Missing/unknown scope ko deny-by-default rakho. Server-side permissions ke saath Android checks ko bhi align karo.

